[Music]  Kito is more than a thousand kilometers  from Venezuela yet some people who have  fled the chaos there have made it to the  Ecuadorian capital on foot about 50 of  them live in this compound at the edge  of the city and keep busy tending the  kitchen garden when I water the garden I  always think of my house back home in  Venezuela we used to water the garden  every evening it relieves the stress  this garden was set up with the help of  the city backed participatory urban  agriculture program a group are so far  the gardener's eat what they grow it's a  step towards self-sufficiency Jose sorry  Yaga and his family fled Venezuela last  autumn they threatened me and said we  should leave the country because we were  not cooperative and were therefore  traitors the comb here stands in stark  contrast to the commotion and crisis  back home coming here the Santeria has  left everything and everyone behind  I left Venezuela with my wife and Marie  and the four of us we have a house there  and we let the opposition use it while  they were preparing for the elections  the refugees here work together in the  garden they help each other  some already know about farming and can  instruct the others you plant a seed  here my group are has had an impressive  impact with its participatory approach  involving the most vulnerable groups are  friends from Venezuela need help with  this project we can offer support to  migrants and refugees in our country so  they can find some stability and  integrate in our society this is just  one of 4000 urban farming plots across  Quito supported by a group are ranging  from a few square meters on a rooftop to  laura geodes at the edge of town the aim  in each case is to ease access to  healthy food for poor and marginalized  people the greenery also helps improve  air quality and quality of life the  increase in biodiversity in the area  reduces urban heat islands creates new  spaces and micro climates in which  people can have a better life over the  past four decades Quito has grown almost  fivefold the population has tripled to  more than two and a half million a group  ah helps further food security job  creation environmental management social  inclusion and gender equality  Yano grande used to be a tranquil  village until it was swallowed up by the  city but some open spaces survived and  can be used to grow food a group of  women run this urban farm their work  marks a courageous break with tradition  the early phase of our organization was  very difficult because we had to deal  with them but she's more of our husbands  know we shouldn't do it it was a waste  of time  but it was a spoke of but little by  little we proved that our value as women  goes beyond being mothers we can do all  kinds of things including tending the  land and feeding our families we can  contribute economically I grew up our  staff regularly visit and bring advice  on how to run their ventures - das  there's always more to learn about  farming methods and increasing the  yields oh what a lovely garden a grouper  is committed to organic farming without  chemical fertilizers the women of Yano  grande grow so much produce now they  have some to sell promoting organic  farming the project has also helped  create skills especially among women  that allow them to make a living through  agriculture there's the liquid or the  urban farmers can sell their crops at  special organic produce markets boeuf  arias when we started we didn't even  know how to do the bookkeeping but now  we know more we're learning to grow as  individuals and above all we're partners  in the community urban gardening  projects around the world book - a group  are as a model the pioneering  organization is continuing to expand in  Quito the area devoted to urban farming  here in Quito is growing about 3  hectares or about 200 different plots  each year for the refugees from  Venezuela urban farming might prove the  first step to a new life

